course: english
lesson: 18
title: Inference: reading between the lines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Inference means working out what the writer means but does not say. «Lily slammed the door and threw her bag on the floor.» The text never says «angry», but slamming and throwing are clues, and the clues show it. A good reader is a detective: find the clues, then say what they show.
explain: A detective does not need a magnifying glass, only careful eyes. Every inference needs a reason from the text: «Jack was nervous because his hands shook as he opened the letter.» Say the idea, then the clue, joined with «because». That is a two-mark answer.
explain: An inference must fit ALL the clues, not just one. «Grandma's eyes twinkled as she hid the parcel behind her back.» Is she a thief? Twinkling eyes mean fun and mischief, so she is probably hiding a present. Feelings can be mixed too: «Maya smiled, but her eyes stayed sad» means she is hiding a sad feeling behind a smile.
explain: Your own life helps. Owen in Swansea says his mam calls him «tamping» (Welsh English for furious) when he slams doors, so he knows how the girl in the story feels. That is a good start, but always check your idea against the clues in the text.
fact: Inference means working out something the text suggests but does not say directly.
fact: A clue is a detail in the text, like an action or a description, that hints at something.
fact: A good inference gives the idea and the evidence: «She was angry because she slammed the door.»
fact: An inference must fit all the clues, not just one of them.
fact: Inference is reasoning from clues, not wild guessing.
fact: Characters can have mixed feelings, like smiling while sad inside.
fact: Descriptions of a place can tell you about the people, like a weedy garden and a broken gate suggesting nobody cares for the house.
fact: Some inference questions have more than one sensible answer, as long as each fits the clues.
fact: Precise words are better than vague ones: «stormy and wet» says more than «bad».
fact: Linking a story to your own life helps, but the text has the final say.
fact: Slang: «tamping» (Wales) means furious; «lowkey» means a bit, or secretly; «it's giving bitter» (Gen Z) means it seems bitter.
exercise: «Lily slammed the door and threw her bag on the floor.» How was Lily feeling? | Angry | Sleepy and calm after a long, quiet day at school | Happy | Excited about the weekend
exercise: «Jack's hands shook as he opened the letter.» Ruby writes «nervous because his hands shook». Why is this a good answer? | It gives a feeling and the clue that shows it | It copies the whole sentence from the text word for word | It is short and has no reason | It guesses what the letter says
exercise: «Grandma's eyes twinkled as she hid the parcel behind her back.» Ollie says, «She's a thief!» What fits the clues best? | She's hiding a present as a nice surprise | She stole the parcel from a neighbour and is afraid of being caught | She is cross with the children | She has lost the parcel
exercise: Owen calls the girl in the story «tamping». What does he mean? | Furious | Tired out from running about | Very quiet | Wet from the rain
exercise: «The hall was silent. Every head turned towards Ben as he stood up.» What does this suggest? | Something important is about to happen | The hall was empty and everyone had already gone home | Ben was asleep | It was very noisy
exercise: «Maya smiled, but her eyes stayed sad.» Is Maya happy? | Not really, she's hiding sadness behind a smile | Yes, smiling always means a person is happy inside | Yes, she is laughing at a joke | No, she is angry with her friend
exercise: Tess says the character is «lowkey jealous». What does lowkey mean? | A bit, or secretly | Very loudly | Not at all | In a low voice only
exercise: «The garden was full of weeds, and the gate hung on one hinge.» What can you tell about the house? | Nobody has looked after it for a long time | The owners are proud gardeners who work hard every weekend | It is brand new | It is a busy shop
exercise: «Dad looked at the clock again and sighed.» Why might he sigh? | He's waiting for someone who is late | He has just won a big prize in a competition and can't believe it | He loves the clock | He is fast asleep
exercise: Grace's teacher says, «Inference is like being a detective.» What does she mean? | Look closely for clues and work out what they show | Buy a magnifying glass before you start reading any book | Make up an answer quickly | Ask someone else for the answer
exercise: «The wind howled and rain lashed the windows. Inside, the children huddled by the fire.» Ava writes «Bad.» What is a better answer? | Stormy, windy and very wet | Bad | Sunny and warm with a gentle, pleasant breeze | Snowy
exercise: Which of these is an inference, not a fact stated in the text? | Lily was angry | Lily slammed the door | Lily threw her bag on the floor, which the text says straight out | Lily had a bag
exercise: «It's giving bitter,» says Tess. What does she mean? | It seems bitter, or resentful | It tastes like a lemon | It is a present | It is very kind and generous to everyone
exercise: What is the best way to start a two-mark inference answer? | Give your idea, then «because» and a clue | Copy the question out | Write «I don't know because the text doesn't say it anywhere at all» | Draw a picture
exercise: Can your own life help with inference? | Yes, but check your idea against the clues | No, your own life has nothing to do with books and should be kept out | Yes, and the text doesn't matter | Only if you are a grown-up
test: «she slammed the door so she's just tired innit». What's wrong with this inference? | It doesn't fit the clue: slamming shows anger, not tiredness | Nothing, all slamming means tiredness | It's too long | Doors can't be slammed in stories
test: Ollie: «Grandma's hiding something, she's a criminal, 100%.» Which clue does Ollie ignore? | Her eyes twinkled, which shows fun, not guilt | She was holding a parcel in her hands | She is a grandmother, and grandmothers never hide anything | She was standing in a room
test: «inference = guessing, so any answer's fine, yeah?» What's true? | No, inference is reasoning from clues in the text | Yes, inference is just guessing | Yes, any answer gets the marks in an inference question as long as it is long | No, inference is copying the words
test: ★★ «Ella grinned and hugged the envelope. Then she ran to tell her mum.» What was probably in the envelope? | Good news | A bill for the gas | Bad news from school that she wanted to hide from everyone | Nothing at all
test: ★★ «Dad looked at the clock again and sighed. The pizza was going cold.» Which is the best inference? | Someone is late for tea and Dad is fed up waiting | Dad is happy that the pizza is cooling down nicely | Dad has broken the clock and is upset | Dad is asleep at the table
test: ★★★ «Noah's coat was soaked, but his hair was perfectly dry. He shook out his umbrella in the hall.» What can you infer? | It was raining and his umbrella kept his head dry | It was sunny all day and he had been swimming in the sea | His coat fell in the bath at home | He forgot his umbrella at school
test: ★★★ «Every seat on the bus was empty except one. Priya sat beside the old man anyway, and he smiled for the first time that day.» What does this show about Priya? | She was kind: she chose to sit with him when she didn't have to | She had to sit there because there was nowhere else to sit on the bus | She was lost | She didn't see the empty seats
test: In a cartoon, a character's face turns red and steam comes out of its ears. What feeling does this show? | Anger | Cold | Boredom | Hunger after missing lunch at school
test: «Maya smiled so she's fine, case closed». What would a good detective say? | Her sad eyes show she's hiding how she feels | Smiles never lie, so she must be absolutely fine and happy | She's laughing at Tess | We can't know anything from faces
test: What would happen if you answered every inference question with the feeling but no clue? | You'd miss marks, because the evidence is missing | You'd get full marks every single time | The teacher would give you extra marks for being short | Nothing
test: Gran in the story says the house is «a right state» (northern for a real mess). Ruby infers the garden is messy. What would make her answer stronger? | Adding the clue: weeds and a broken gate | Writing «a right state» three times | Saying it in a posh voice | Making up a new clue that isn't in the text but sounds good
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises inference in reading? | The deduction of meaning not explicitly stated, on the basis of evidence within the text | The verbatim reproduction of words appearing in the text | Unsupported conjecture | Recollection of facts from beyond the text in question
exam: It is stated that a character's hands trembled as he opened a letter. Identify the inference best supported by this evidence. | That the character was nervous | That the character was cold, the weather being wintry and the house unheated | That the character was asleep | That the letter was empty
exam: Ascertain which of the following constitutes a complete two-mark response to an inference question. | A statement of the inferred idea together with supporting evidence from the text | A statement of the idea alone | A copy of the question | A description of one's own feelings unconnected with the text whatsoever
exam: Given that a character smiles whilst her eyes remain sad, select the response consistent with the evidence. | She conceals an unhappy feeling behind an outward smile | She is wholly content | She is angry | She is unable to see clearly owing to bright sunlight
